  "description": "Select the fastest validated population-count and fused bit-reduction paths across the target host's input sizes and cache regimes.\n\n## Background\n\nAVX2 kernels already use a nibble lookup with VPSHUFB and VPSADBW, including AND-popcount. Compare that implementation with hardware scalar POPCNT and Harley-Seal carry-save accumulation. More Boolean instructions or wider batches alone do not establish a benefit for short or memory-bound inputs.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n- [hard] REQ-01: Satisfy the [Zen 3 measurement contract](dev/active/1a379447-zen3-cpu-performance/measurement-contract.md) for correctness, reproducible release benchmarks, comparison validity and adoption; receipts pin its committed version, the frozen protocol and the cell addendum. Include mandatory before/after evidence for production changes and preserve negative outcomes.\n\n- [hard] REQ-07: Compare scalar POPCNT, the existing nibble-LUT path and Harley-Seal candidates against pinned libpopcnt and Mula reference implementations on identical buffers. Cover small inputs, vector and CSA-block boundaries, cache-resident arrays, streaming arrays, alignment offsets and zero/dense/random bit patterns.\n\n- [hard] REQ-08: Preserve exact counts, canonical bit indexing and zero tail padding. Exercise fused AND-popcount and any additional fused reduction only where it replaces a measured consumer's temporary buffer or extra pass.\n\n- [hard] REQ-09: Publish cycles per byte, useful bytes per second, per-call latency and representative consumer results with all dispatch/setup costs. Determine and independently confirm size-dependent winners through the canonical tuning mechanism.\n\n- [hard] REQ-10: Inspect register lifetimes, accumulator dependency chains, scalar tails and actual emitted instructions. Report no-win regions and retain their established implementation rather than making Harley-Seal unconditional.\n\n\n\n\n## Notes\n\nSource references: [Libpopcnt2026], [Mula2018].",
